  "question": {
    "text": "In a meter bridge experiment resistances are connected as shown in the figure. Initially resistance <math><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4&#160;</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math> and the neutral point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> is at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> . Now an unknown resistance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is connected in series to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>&nbsp;and the new position of the neutral point is at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>80</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> . The value of unknown resistance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;is -<br /><br /><img src=\"assets\\qt0313_54aa9f851cc3.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 252px;\" />"
  },
